And your paired design is appropriate for this kind of comparison: using the same stochastic input for both alternatives is a standard common-random-numbers / paired simulation technique, which can make differences between alternatives easier to estimate because both conditions experience the same random disturbance. 

But there is an important warning

Don't write:

> “v3.6 is worse than v3.5 because lifespan decreased by 0.2.”



That would overinterpret the result.

Your MC output explicitly says:

> “No meaningful lifespan difference detected (Δ=-0.20, n=50).”



So the defensible conclusion is:

> Under the MC-v0.3 configuration tested (50 paired runs, seed 42, maximum 50 ticks), v3.6 introduced measurable Active Shielding behavior that was absent in v3.5. It increased average stability and reduced breach events, but did not produce a meaningful improvement in lifespan or collapse count in this experiment.



That's actually a very good experimental finding, because it tells you what the next experiment needs to investigate rather than pretending the architecture is proven.

